title Bulk Customer Create — Durability Spike with db-scheduler

|Client|
start
:**PRECONDITION**\n· POST /api/customers\n· Set<CustomerRequestDto> (JSON array,\n  not a file upload); <<PRE>>

|CustomerBulkResource|
:BulkResource.create(dtos);
:Delegate to CustomerBulkService.create(items);

|BulkService|
:**PRECONDITION**\n· Set<CustomerRequestDto> items; <<PRE>>
:validateAll(items)\nvia StructuredTaskScope\n(virtual threads, fan-out — one\nsubtask per item, joined together);

fork
  :Validate item #1;
fork again
  :Validate item #N;
end fork

if (any item invalid?) then (yes)
  :Collect ApiFieldError list\nfor invalid items;
else (no)
endif

|BulkService|
:processValid(validItems)\nvia a second StructuredTaskScope\n(virtual threads, fan-out);

fork
  :onValidCreate(dto #1);
fork again
  :onValidCreate(dto #N);
end fork

|CustomerBulkService|
:onValidCreate(dto);
:CustomerBulkCreateDispatcher.schedule(dto);

|CustomerBulkCreateDispatcher|
:**PRECONDITION**\n· CustomerRequestDto dto\n· OneTimeTask<CustomerRequestDto>\n  (CustomerBulkCreateTask); <<PRE>>
:scheduler.scheduleIfNotExists(\n  customerBulkCreateTask\n    .schedulableInstance(dto.getId(), dto));

|db-scheduler|
:Persist task instance in\nPostgres table scheduling.scheduled_tasks\n(BEFORE the HTTP response is returned); <<POST>>
note right
  db-scheduler polls its own table every 2s and executes due tasks
  asynchronously — completely decoupled from the HTTP request thread.

  **Survives a server restart**: the task is persisted in
  scheduled_tasks, not in memory. Heartbeat mechanism (10s interval,
  4 missed heartbeats) detects a "dead" execution — e.g. the server
  crashed mid-task — and automatically revives it on another node.
end note

|BulkService|
:**POSTCONDITION**\n· All valid items durably scheduled\n· invalid items collected as errors; <<POST>>

|CustomerBulkResource|
:✔  ApiResult.bulk(errors, processed, failed); <<SUCCESS>>

|Client|
:HTTP response returned immediately —\nactual processing happens later, durably;
stop

@enduml
